WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.267 --> 00:00:02.535
-Oh, you know what
I wanted to mention here?

00:00:02.535 --> 00:00:04.337
Because there's a connection
between "Reservoir Dogs"

00:00:04.337 --> 00:00:07.173
and "The Golden Girls,"
involving you.

00:00:07.173 --> 00:00:09.375
-Sorta kinda.
-Sorta kinda.

00:00:09.375 --> 00:00:10.543
I don't know if people
knew about this.

00:00:10.543 --> 00:00:12.044
What is the connection?

00:00:12.044 --> 00:00:17.484
-Okay, the connection was,
before I did "Reservoir Dogs,"

00:00:17.484 --> 00:00:21.087
I had a very unsuccessful
acting career.

00:00:21.087 --> 00:00:24.190
However, one of the few jobs
I did get --

00:00:24.190 --> 00:00:25.591
Is this the camera?
This is the camera.

00:00:25.591 --> 00:00:26.959
-Yeah.
-Yeah, one of the few jobs

00:00:26.959 --> 00:00:28.328
I did get --
[ Laughter ]

00:00:28.328 --> 00:00:30.329
-I love that Quentin Tarantino
just directed our show.

00:00:30.329 --> 00:00:31.564
-Yeah!
[ Laughter ]

00:00:31.564 --> 00:00:33.099
That's the best thing
that ever happened to us!

00:00:33.099 --> 00:00:34.767
-Camera two!
-This is fantastic!

00:00:34.767 --> 00:00:36.435
This is best thing
to ever happen to us!

00:00:36.435 --> 00:00:38.404
So happy.
Oh, my God.

00:00:38.404 --> 00:00:40.806
That is awesome.
-Okay, slow move in.

00:00:40.806 --> 00:00:42.808
[ Laughter ]
Slow, I mean, really like --

00:00:42.808 --> 00:00:44.811
-Slow move in, yeah, come on.
-Like, get dramatic.

00:00:44.811 --> 00:00:46.011
-Yeah, that's -- all right.

00:00:46.011 --> 00:00:50.016
-Uh...
[ Laughter ]

00:00:50.016 --> 00:00:51.784
What were we talking about?
Oh, yeah.

00:00:51.784 --> 00:00:53.319
-"Golden Girls."
-Okay, yeah, okay.

00:00:53.319 --> 00:00:55.121
So, I had a very unsuccessful
acting career.

00:00:55.121 --> 00:00:57.957
[ Laughter ]
However, one of the jobs

00:00:57.957 --> 00:01:00.960
I did get, and not because
I did a wonderful audition,

00:01:00.960 --> 00:01:04.230
but simply because they sent
my picture in, and they said,

00:01:04.230 --> 00:01:07.666
"He's got it,"
was for an Elvis impersonator

00:01:07.666 --> 00:01:09.235
on "The Golden Girls."
[ Laughter ]

00:01:09.235 --> 00:01:10.670
-Now, were you into Elvis
at the time?

00:01:10.670 --> 00:01:13.773
-Oh, yeah! Oh, I was, like --
Well, that's why it worked.

00:01:13.773 --> 00:01:17.910
Because I walked around
dressed like Elvis in the '80s.

00:01:17.910 --> 00:01:20.680
I wore a pompadour all the time.
-Really?

00:01:20.680 --> 00:01:23.883
-I actually went to a rockabilly
place to get my hair cut.

00:01:23.883 --> 00:01:25.585
-Yes!
-Like, the people that cut

00:01:25.585 --> 00:01:27.186
the Stray Cats' hair...
-Brian Setzer.

00:01:27.186 --> 00:01:29.021
-Yeah, and the people that cut
The Blasters' hair,

00:01:29.021 --> 00:01:30.089
they cut my [bleep] hair.

00:01:30.089 --> 00:01:31.223
Or, they cut my hair.
Sorry.

00:01:31.223 --> 00:01:32.825
-It's all right, we got you.
[ Laughter ]

00:01:32.825 --> 00:01:33.860
We can bleep you out,
no worries.

00:01:33.860 --> 00:01:35.194
Yeah, no problem.
-Yeah.

00:01:35.194 --> 00:01:37.130
So I literally got from, like,
rockabilly professionals,

00:01:37.130 --> 00:01:38.297
I got my hair done that way.

00:01:38.297 --> 00:01:40.667
And the black shoe polish
going on,

00:01:40.667 --> 00:01:42.601
and I wore houndstooth coats

00:01:42.601 --> 00:01:44.103
and dressed like
a hillbilly cat.

00:01:44.103 --> 00:01:46.171
-Wow.
-And, um --

00:01:46.171 --> 00:01:48.307
And so I did this
"Golden Girls."

00:01:48.307 --> 00:01:50.443
And it's like Sophia's wedding
is what it was.

00:01:50.443 --> 00:01:51.911
And the thing is,
we were like a --

00:01:51.911 --> 00:01:53.680
We have a chorus at the end.

00:01:53.680 --> 00:01:56.882
It became a two-part
"Golden Girls."

00:01:56.882 --> 00:02:00.119
So I got paid residuals
for both parts.

00:02:00.119 --> 00:02:03.989
All right?
And it was so popular,

00:02:03.989 --> 00:02:06.826
they put it on
a "Best of 'The Golden Girls,'"

00:02:06.826 --> 00:02:09.929
and I got residuals
every time that showed.

00:02:09.929 --> 00:02:13.132
So I got paid maybe --
I don't know --

00:02:13.132 --> 00:02:16.369
$650 for the episode.
-Yeah.

00:02:16.369 --> 00:02:21.874
-But by the time the residuals
were over, three years later,

00:02:21.874 --> 00:02:25.878
I made like $3,000.
-Wow!

00:02:25.878 --> 00:02:27.313
[ Laughter ]

00:02:27.313 --> 00:02:31.284
-And that kept me going
during our preproduction time

00:02:31.284 --> 00:02:32.652
trying to get
"Reservoir Dogs" going.

00:02:32.652 --> 00:02:34.920
-So thank "Golden Girls"
for "Reservoir Dogs."

00:02:34.920 --> 00:02:39.359
I mean, that's what I'm saying.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:02:39.359 --> 00:02:41.627
It's a shame
we don't have the clip,

00:02:41.627 --> 00:02:42.861
because I would love to show --

00:02:42.861 --> 00:02:44.197
Oh, wait, we do have it.
-Oh!

00:02:44.197 --> 00:02:46.666
-Here's Quentin Tarantino
as an Elvis impersonator

00:02:46.666 --> 00:02:49.835
on "The Golden Girls."
Check this out.

00:02:49.835 --> 00:02:52.104
-♪ I want you ♪

00:02:52.104 --> 00:02:54.908
♪ I love you ♪

00:02:54.908 --> 00:02:59.912
♪ With all my heart ♪

00:02:59.912 --> 00:03:01.547
-That's what I'm talking about.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:03:01.547 --> 00:03:03.315
You got a big laugh.
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:03:03.315 --> 00:03:04.884
Yeah, and by the way,
you will notice,

00:03:04.884 --> 00:03:08.554
I'm not like the Vegas guy
in the long -- in the jumpsuit.

00:03:08.554 --> 00:03:11.690
-That's too obvious.
-I'm the Sun Records Elvis.

00:03:11.690 --> 00:03:13.526
I am absolutely
the Sun Record --

00:03:13.526 --> 00:03:14.860
And I'm totally doing my --

00:03:14.860 --> 00:03:17.030
♪ Got a lot of living to do ♪

00:03:17.030 --> 00:03:20.265
♪ Got a lot of
loving to do, oh, baby ♪

00:03:20.265 --> 00:03:22.301
♪ Baby ♪

00:03:22.301 --> 00:03:24.304
Yeah, I'm singing my own song,

00:03:24.304 --> 00:03:25.705
regardless of
what they're singing.

00:03:25.705 --> 00:03:26.872
-I love that.

00:03:26.872 --> 00:03:28.107
By the way,
happy birthday, Elvis.

00:03:28.107 --> 00:03:29.375
Today is his birthday.
-Happy birthday, Elvis!

00:03:29.375 --> 00:03:32.444
-Today is his birthday.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:03:32.444 --> 00:03:34.914
I was showing this DVD earlier,

00:03:34.914 --> 00:03:37.016
and I've never seen
a box like this.

00:03:37.016 --> 00:03:39.051
I almost -- for a second, I
thought it was an eight-track.

00:03:39.051 --> 00:03:41.020
-Yeah, it does look --
-Right?

00:03:41.020 --> 00:03:42.688
-Yeah, I --
Wow, I didn't even --

00:03:42.688 --> 00:03:45.291
Until you put it on, I didn't
realize we have a black thing.

00:03:45.291 --> 00:03:46.892
-Yeah.
It's pretty cool.

00:03:46.892 --> 00:03:48.394
-A black-ray.
-You should be --

00:03:48.394 --> 00:03:50.196
-You should have
the soundtrack on eight-track.

00:03:50.196 --> 00:03:51.363
-I agree.

00:03:51.363 --> 00:03:53.232
-The soundtrack is crusher,
by the way.

00:03:53.232 --> 00:03:54.900
-Thank you very much.
-They're always great.

00:03:54.900 --> 00:03:56.268
You -- I don't know how you --

00:03:56.268 --> 00:03:57.970
How do you pick these songs
for your movies?

00:03:57.970 --> 00:04:00.072
'Cause they're awesome,
and I almost don't --

00:04:00.072 --> 00:04:01.607
I go, "Oh, I love that song,"

00:04:01.607 --> 00:04:03.142
or, "I've never
heard that song."

00:04:03.142 --> 00:04:04.610
-Well, you know, part of
the thing is, it's like,

00:04:04.610 --> 00:04:05.777
when I'm coming up
with a film --

00:04:05.777 --> 00:04:07.446
See, I have -- I'm lucky.

00:04:07.446 --> 00:04:13.953
Where it's like -- I have
a whole vinyl room in my house.

00:04:13.953 --> 00:04:16.456
Where it's like, it's all done
up like a used-record store.

00:04:16.456 --> 00:04:18.324
-Wow.
-And all broken down

00:04:18.324 --> 00:04:19.492
into categories and everything

00:04:19.492 --> 00:04:20.960
so you can
thumb through everything.

00:04:20.960 --> 00:04:22.094
And I've got, like,
movie posters

00:04:22.094 --> 00:04:23.796
that have to do with movies
on the wall.

00:04:23.796 --> 00:04:25.565
I have my platinum records
up there.

00:04:25.565 --> 00:04:26.965
Just saying.
-No big deal.

00:04:26.965 --> 00:04:30.469
-Just sayin', just sayin'.
-Yeah, me too.

00:04:30.469 --> 00:04:32.538
-But part of
the thing is, though,

00:04:32.538 --> 00:04:35.608
when I'm thinking about
a new movie,

00:04:35.608 --> 00:04:37.877
I go through
my record collection,

00:04:37.877 --> 00:04:42.748
and I start playing music that
could be right for the film.

00:04:42.748 --> 00:04:45.017
And then,
if I find enough of it,

00:04:45.017 --> 00:04:48.187
that actually makes me
move to the next step,

00:04:48.187 --> 00:04:49.722
the next part of the process.

00:04:49.722 --> 00:04:50.989
All right, so, like,
it's like, uh --

00:04:50.989 --> 00:04:52.358
Like something in the case
of something like

00:04:52.358 --> 00:04:53.959
"Django Unchained,"

00:04:53.959 --> 00:04:55.861
I'm thinking about
spaghetti Western music.

00:04:55.861 --> 00:04:57.863
And so I'm playing my spaghetti
Western soundtracks.

00:04:57.863 --> 00:04:59.498
And when I finally
find enough of them

00:04:59.498 --> 00:05:00.799
that actually
sound interesting --

00:05:00.799 --> 00:05:02.534
And then I'm playing
my '70s soul soundtracks

00:05:02.534 --> 00:05:04.102
because that needs --
for something like

00:05:04.102 --> 00:05:06.572
"Django Unchained," that needs
to fit itself into there.

00:05:06.572 --> 00:05:09.442
So when I find a couple of them,
I go, "Hey!

00:05:09.442 --> 00:05:11.477
I might have a movie here.
This could be interesting."

00:05:11.477 --> 00:05:12.811
Same thing with "Jackie Brown."

00:05:12.811 --> 00:05:16.315
I went, like, just deep diving
into '70s soul music

00:05:16.315 --> 00:05:18.650
and exploitation soundtracks,

00:05:18.650 --> 00:05:20.686
like women-prison movie
soundtracks and everything.

00:05:20.686 --> 00:05:22.321
And then,
by putting those together,

00:05:22.321 --> 00:05:24.356
it's like, "Oh, hey,
I think I could have a movie."

00:05:24.356 --> 00:05:26.659
So it's like --
It's not what the movie's about,

00:05:26.659 --> 00:05:28.861
but it's sort of the kind of

00:05:28.861 --> 00:05:30.563
the walls of
the gingerbread house...

00:05:30.563 --> 00:05:31.831
-Yeah.
-...to some degree or another.

00:05:31.831 --> 00:05:33.665
-Is the movie written
by the time you're picking

00:05:33.665 --> 00:05:36.102
these songs or --
-No, that's usually, like,

00:05:36.102 --> 00:05:38.504
me thinking about doing it.
All right?

00:05:38.504 --> 00:05:40.006
Like, okay, like,
"Oh, this might be

00:05:40.006 --> 00:05:41.440
the next thing
I sit down and write."

00:05:41.440 --> 00:05:43.643
So maybe I've written,
like, the first scene.

00:05:43.643 --> 00:05:44.977
-Yeah.
-I've done it enough

00:05:44.977 --> 00:05:47.180
for it to be more than an idea.
-Yeah.

00:05:47.180 --> 00:05:50.783
-But I haven't committed 100% to
"This is what I'm doing next."

00:05:50.783 --> 00:05:53.419
And so I've committed enough
to writing, like,

00:05:53.419 --> 00:05:57.022
maybe the first scene or, like,
a fragment of a sequence,

00:05:57.022 --> 00:05:59.892
or some dialogue back and forth
between a character.

00:05:59.892 --> 00:06:03.061
And then I go into the deep dive
to figure out the --

00:06:03.061 --> 00:06:05.030
figure out the movie sonically.

00:06:05.030 --> 00:06:07.766
-Well, you've set the bar
for soundtracks

00:06:07.766 --> 00:06:10.335
and for films, in general.
We love you, buddy.

00:06:10.335 --> 00:06:11.670
-Thank you so much, Jimmy.
Appreciate it.

00:06:11.670 --> 00:06:13.839
That is Quentin Tarantino.
-My first time here!

00:06:13.839 --> 00:06:15.541
-"Once Upon a Time
in Hollywood."

00:06:15.541 --> 00:06:17.043
First time on the show!

